        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ज्वालयति",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ज्वालयति — jvālayati — \"lights; kindles\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "माता सायं दीपं ज्वालयति। mātā sāyaṃ dīpaṃ jvālayati. Mother lights a lamp in the evening."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Past (she …): सा दीपं ज्वालितवती, sā dīpaṃ jvālitavatī."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ज्वालयति (jvālayati) is built on the root √jval, \"to blaze\", which gives jvalati, \"burns\"; MW gives its causative, \"makes blaze\", as to set on fire, light or kindle, and KRM gives the causative participle jvālita (MW)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for getting around."
